# ==========================================================================
#  O05  A particular or a kind
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    Wigtown, The Inkwell and Rhona Blackwood are particulars; Settlement,
#    Bookshop and Person are kinds; rdf:type is the link, and a kind is a
#    referent with an IRI like any other.
#
#  WHY
#    Some symbols in the brief pick out one thing: Wigtown, The Inkwell,
#    Rhona Blackwood, the Lodestone Group. Others pick out a kind of thing:
#    town, shop, author, publisher. The first are particulars, called
#    individuals in OWL; the second are kinds, called classes. The relation
#    between them is rdf:type, and it is the first relation worth writing
#    down, because most of the brief's questions are questions about kinds:
#    which shops, which towns, which events.
#
#    A kind is a referent too. bs:Settlement refers to the kind, town, in
#    the same way bt:place-wigtown refers to the town, and it gets an IRI
#    on the same terms. The IRI is what lets you say things about the kind
#    itself: what it is called in Welsh, what its definition is, which
#    other kinds it falls under. The class hierarchy of module 02 is just
#    statements about kinds.
#
#    The line between particular and kind is not always where the brief
#    puts it. 'Crime fiction' looks like a kind of book, and a class of
#    works is one fair reading. It is also a thing a shop specialises in,
#    and a shop is not a member of a class of books. The Trail treats
#    genres as particulars, concepts in a scheme, so that both a work and a
#    shop can point at the same one. o24 returns to that choice; it is the
#    most consequential one in the course.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       particulars (individuals)          rdf:type        kinds (classes)
#
#       bt:place-wigtown        ------------------------>  bs:Settlement
#       bt:shop-inkwell         ------------------------>  bs:Bookshop
#       bt:author-rhona-blackwood --------------------->  bs:Person
#       bt:genre-crime-fiction  ------------------------>  skos:Concept   <- a particular, on purpose
#
#       Both columns are referents with IRIs. The right-hand ones can be
#       labelled, defined and arranged under each other, like anything else.
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - rdf:type says which kind a particular belongs to. It is the first
#      structural statement, and the one every question about 'which
#      shops' depends on.
#    - A class is a referent, with an IRI, a label and a definition.
#      Saying things about kinds is what a taxonomy is.
#    - Whether something is a particular or a kind is a decision, and
#      genre is the case where the obvious answer is not the best one.
#
#  CORNER    referent
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  LOAD IT   https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F01-meaning%2Fo05-a-particular-or-a-kind.ttl
#  SOURCES   RDF Schema 3.3 rdf:type
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-schema/#ch_type
#            RDF Schema 2.2 rdfs:Class
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf12-schema/#ch_class
#  MEASURED  parses, 17 triples; 3 classes, 1 concepts, 4 individuals; 'Every particular and its kin' 4 rows
# ==========================================================================

@prefix bt:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/> .
@prefix bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

bs:Settlement
    a             rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label    "Settlement"@en ;
    rdfs:comment  "A town or city."@en .

bs:Bookshop
    a             rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label    "Bookshop"@en ;
    rdfs:comment  "A business that sells books from premises."@en .

bs:Person
    a             rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label    "Person"@en ;
    rdfs:comment  "A human being."@en .

bt:place-wigtown
    a           bs:Settlement ;
    rdfs:label  "Wigtown"@en .

bt:shop-inkwell
    a           bs:Bookshop ;
    rdfs:label  "The Inkwell"@en .

bt:author-rhona-blackwood
    a           bs:Person ;
    rdfs:label  "Rhona Blackwood"@en .

# A genre is a particular here, not a class: something a shop specialises in
# and a work belongs to. Module 04 and module 06 make the case in full.
bt:genre-crime-fiction
    a           skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label  "Crime Fiction"@en .


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- Every particular and its kind  (on the model)
#  PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
#  SELECT ?thing ?kind WHERE { ?thing a ?kind . FILTER(?kind != rdfs:Class) } ORDER BY ?kind
